# Both app Dockerfiles do `COPY . .` in their build stage, so this file is the ONLY thing
# deciding what leaves the developer's machine. Without it the build context was the whole
# working tree: ~1.3G node_modules, ~2.3G apps/mobile/ios, ~2.4G apps/mobile/android, .git —
# and, worse than the size, `.env.local`, which .env.example documents as the local secret
# store. A secret that reaches any image layer has leaked, even if a later stage discards it.
#
# Deny-nothing-by-default is deliberate here: the build needs the sources, and enumerating
# what to KEEP would silently drop a new package the day it is created.

# Secrets — never, under any name. A credential that reaches any image layer has leaked, so the
# list covers the shapes a credential takes here: the env files, the key and certificate
# extensions, and the signed Temporal token, which is a bare file with no extension to catch.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p8
*.p12
*.keystore
.temporal-token
.temporal-token.*
google-services.json
GoogleService-Info.plist

# Installed and built output. pnpm install runs inside the image; a host node_modules is both
# huge and wrong (platform-specific binaries, a different lockfile resolution).
node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
.next
**/.next
*.tsbuildinfo
**/*.tsbuildinfo
.turbo
**/.turbo

# Native mobile toolchains — not part of any server image, and the two largest trees here.
apps/mobile/ios
apps/mobile/android

# History, tooling and local state.
.git
.gitignore
.github
.claude
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
coverage
*.log

# Docs. The server images render none of it, and docs/ alone is a large tree of markdown.
docs
README.md

# Local material no image needs: the design exports read from disk at build time by a person,
# and the pinned tools a gate downloads. Both are large and neither is application code.
HelioGrid-UX
.tools
